# mysql-patterns (Cursor)

Check the engine and exact version first (`SELECT VERSION()`) — MySQL and
MariaDB diverge on several details, most notably `ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE`
row-alias syntax (MySQL) vs `VALUES(col)` (MariaDB, still deprecated-but-
supported on MySQL for cross-engine compatibility).

## Schema defaults

`BIGINT UNSIGNED AUTO_INCREMENT` for surrogate keys on tables that can grow
large; `DECIMAL` for money, never `FLOAT`/`DOUBLE`; `utf8mb4` everywhere,
not `utf8`/`utf8mb3`; `DATETIME` with UTC managed by the app; soft deletes
via `deleted_at DATETIME NULL` with a scoped index; a lookup table or
constrained `VARCHAR` over `ENUM` for values that change often.

## Indexing

Composite index order: equality predicates first, then range/sort columns.
Always run `EXPLAIN` before adding or changing an index — watch for `type:
ALL` on a large table, `key: NULL` despite a selective predicate, high
`rows`, or `Using temporary`/`Using filesort` in `Extra`. Don't add indexes
blindly — each one costs write throughput, migration time, and buffer-pool
pressure.

## Query patterns

Upsert via `ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE` with `VALUES(col)` for cross-engine
code, or the row-alias form only once the target is confirmed MySQL.
Keyset pagination (`WHERE (created_at, id) < (?, ?) ORDER BY ... LIMIT`)
backed by a matching composite index — never deep `OFFSET` on large
tables. JSON columns for extension data only; expose a generated+indexed
column for any JSON path queried frequently, keep FK/ownership/tenancy
fields relational. Full-text search via `FULLTEXT` index +
`MATCH...AGAINST`; reach for external search when typo-tolerance or
cross-table ranking is needed.

## Transactions

Keep them short, lock rows in a deterministic order across code paths, do
external API calls before opening the transaction not inside it, add
indexes for predicates used in locking reads/updates/deletes, retry on
deadlock with a bounded budget, capture `SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS\G`
immediately after a deadlock since it's overwritten by later events. Use
`SKIP LOCKED` only for queue-style worker claims, never as a substitute for
normal transactional consistency.

## Connection pools

Keep pool recycle time below the server's `wait_timeout`; enable
`pool_pre_ping`/keepalive to recover from network/failover events.

## Replication

Read replicas can lag — never route read-your-own-write paths, checkout
flows, permission checks, or idempotency-key reads to a replica right
after a write. Monitor SQL/IO thread health and lag, not just the TCP
connection.

## Security

Never grant `ALL PRIVILEGES`/`*.*` to application users; require TLS for
cross-host traffic; separate migration/admin users from runtime users;
credentials in a secret manager, never in code/scripts.

## Anti-patterns

`SELECT *` in hot paths, deep `OFFSET` pagination, no index on FK joins,
long transactions, direct DML against `mysql.user`, an app user with admin
grants, pool recycle above `wait_timeout`, replica reads right after
writes.
